<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Work song · 1866 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">The Song of the Volga Boatmen</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ru">Эй, ухнем!</bdi> / <bdi lang="ru-Latn">Ey, ukhnyem!</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A work song of the men who pulled river barges along the Volga by rope. Its slow, heavy tune is sung by deep bass voices.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (collected and arranged by Mily Balakirev)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>burlaks (barge-haulers) of the Volga, Russia</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Russian</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Volga River, Russia (written down at Nizhny Novgorod)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>collected 1860 or 1861; published 1866</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Work song</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics traditional (one verse printed 1866))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-song-of-the-volga-boatmen">The Song of the Volga Boatmen</a>&quot; is a traditional Russian song of the burlaks, the barge-haulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga River</a>, who dragged boats upstream from the bank. In Russian it is named after its refrain, a hauling call. Ilya Repin's painting Barge Haulers on the Volga shows such burlaks at their labour in Tsarist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>.</p><p>The composer Mily Balakirev wrote the song down from Nikolay Aleynikov in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a> in 1860 or 1861, and published it with his own arrangement in his collection of Russian folk songs in 1866. He printed only one verse, the first; the other two were added later. The first released recording was probably made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1900 by Alexander Makarov-Yunev for Gramophone.</p><p>The bass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodor-chaliapin">Feodor Chaliapin</a> made it famous, in an arrangement by Feodor Koenemann; his version, released several times (1922, 1927, 1936), became one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s most popular songs, and it has been a concert favourite of bass singers ever since. Composers took it up as well. Alexander Glazunov wrote a piece on Balakirev's tune in 1905 and used it for a theme of his symphonic poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stepan-razin">Stenka Razin</a>, and Max Bruch made it the last movement of his Suite on Russian Folk Melodies. In April 1917, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a> had abdicated, Sergei Diaghilev asked Igor Stravinsky to orchestrate it for wind instruments to open a concert that would otherwise have begun with the anthem &quot;God Save the Tsar&quot;; Stravinsky worked through the night, helped by Lord Berners and Ernest Ansermet. Manuel de Falla's arrangement was published in 1922 at the request of the diplomat Ricardo <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baeza">Baeza</a>, who was working with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> to relieve the more than two million Russian refugees displaced and imprisoned during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>; all proceeds went to that relief.</p><p>Abroad the song travelled through singers such as Vladimir Rosing and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-robeson">Paul Robeson</a>, who sang it in translation. In 1941 the Glenn Miller Orchestra released a swing arrangement by Bill Finegan that reached No. 1 on the Billboard pop chart. Because the song was not in copyright, it was not caught by that year's ASCAP boycott, which gave it more radio play. Its opening notes have since been used in films, cartoons and video games, often to suggest heavy toil or gloom, and sometimes with new humorous words.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song for hauling: its slow, even beat lets a team of men pull together on the towline, and its refrain is the call on which they heave. The mood is one of weight and endurance under hard labour on the river.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Russian</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Russian</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Эй, ухнем! Эй, ухнем!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Èj, uxnem! Èj, uxnem!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Hey, let's heave! Hey, let's heave!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Ещё разик, ещё раз!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Eščë razik, eščë raz!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Once more, once again!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">} 2 раза</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">} 2 raza</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>} twice</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Разовьём мы березу́,</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Razovʹëm my berezú,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We shall untwine the birch,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Разовьём мы кудряву́!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Razovʹëm my kudrjavú!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We shall untwine the curly one!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Ай-да, да, ай-да! Ай-да, да, ай-да!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Aj-da, da, aj-da! Aj-da, da, aj-da!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Ai-da, da, ai-da! Ai-da, da, ai-da!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ru">Разовьём мы кудряву́!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ru-Latn">Razovʹëm my kudrjavú!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>We shall untwine the curly one!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional; as collected by Mily Balakirev (published 1866). Text from <a href="https://ru.wikisource.org/wiki/%D0%AD%D0%B9%2C_%D1%83%D1%85%D0%BD%D0%B5%D0%BC%2F%D0%91%D0%B0%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%BA%D0%B8%D1%80%D0%B5%D0%B2_(%D0%A1%D0%9E)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (scientific transliteration).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (6)</summary><ul><li>line 1.3: '} 2 раза' is the edition's repeat sign (the two lines above are sung twice), not a sung line; kept as a label ('} twice').</li><li>lines 2.1, 2.2, 2.4: the acute accents on 'березу́', 'кудряву́' are the edition's stress marks (sung stress moved to the end, for the rhythm); kept in rom as 'ú'.</li><li>line 1.1: 'ухнем' is 'let us heave' with the cry 'ukh' in it: a hauling call, often rendered 'heave-ho'.</li><li>lines 2.1-2.4: 'разовьём' (we shall untwine, unwind, uncurl) and its object are disputed: the folk custom of curling and then uncurling birch branches in the spring rites (Semik), or a working image for unwinding the tow-rope; 'the curly one' is the birch. Rendered literally.</li><li>line 2.3: 'Ai-da, da, ai-da' is a refrain cry with no fixed sense, kept as sound.</li><li>line 2.1: 'березу' is written with е, not ё; romanized as written ('berezú').</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="0tw3g88JtWA" data-title="The Song of the Volga Boatmen: Leonid Kharitonov with the Red Army Choir, live"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Leonid Kharitonov with the Red Army Choir, live</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Leonid Kharitonov (Леонид Харитонов). Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0tw3g88JtWA" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Song of the Volga Boatmen (Glenn Miller Orchestra, arranged by Bill Finegan)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1941</span><span class="sg-chain-h">swing arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Orchestration for wind instruments (Igor Stravinsky)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1917</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Stenka Razin, symphonic poem (Alexander Glazunov)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in (one of its themes)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ey, ukhnyem (Alexander Glazunov)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1905</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Canto de los remeros del Volga (Manuel de Falla)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1922</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Suite on Russian Folk Melodies, Op. 79b, last movement (Max Bruch)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted as</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Májová symfonie, Op. 73 (Vítězslav Novák)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1943</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in (its main motif)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Madame Moscovitch (George Formby)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1934</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in (its first two lines, in English)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Midnight Snack (Tom and Jerry)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1941</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in (a variation)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Stroke (Billy Squier)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1981</span><span class="sg-chain-h">counterpoint</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Punch-Out!! (NES)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in (entry theme of Soda Popinski)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Freo Heave Ho (Fremantle Dockers team song)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1994</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adapted from Stravinsky's arrangement</span></li></ul></section>
